  pages = {291--319},
  issn = {00182702},
  urldate = {2015-01-15},
  abstract = {The article focuses on the idea of economist John F. Muth on rational expectations economics. In addition, it discusses the contributions of Muth on rational expectations literature. Muth has written three papers that develop the rational expectations hypothesis. First, "Rational Expectations and the Theory of Price Movements" was first presented in 1959 and subsequently published in the 1961 issue of "Econometrica." This is the paper that is usually singled out by researchers referring to Muth's hypothesis. Muth's paper advanced the hypothesis that expectations are essentially the same as the predictions of the relevant economic theory. Hence, expectations depend specifically on the structure of the entire system. Moreover, Muth's rational expectations hypothesis asserted three things: Information is scarce, and the economic system generally does not waste it; The way expectations are formed depends specifically on the structure of the relevant system describing the economy; A public prediction will have no substantial effect on the operation of the economic system.},
